1. The dig Utility

docker run --rm --network homelab_lab-network toolbelt/dig dig +short lab-nginx

// Create a temporary container from the toolbelt/dig image and check DNS queries for the lab-nginx container.

Output
172.18.0.2


docker run --rm --network homelab_lab-network toolbelt/dig dig +short google.com

// Check external DNS resolution.

Output
142.251.209.110


P.S.: Running this check from the host will not work for the homelab_lab-network because it is isolated.


2. The Nmap Utility

nmap 172.18.0.2

// Scanning the lab-nginx container. Port 80 is visible as open.

Output
PORT   STATE SERVICE
80/tcp open  http


nmap -p- 172.18.0.2

// Scanning the lab-nginx container across all 65535 ports. Port 80 is visible as open.

Output
PORT   STATE SERVICE
80/tcp open  http


nmap -sV 172.18.0.2

// Scanning the lab-nginx container with version detection. Port 80, the service, and its version are visible.

Output
PORT   STATE SERVICE VERSION
80/tcp open  http    nginx 1.31.8


3. Wireshark

sudo tcpdump -i any -w /tmp/capture.pcap

// Capture traffic and save it to a file in a Wireshark-compatible format.

curl -v http://172.18.0.2

// Send HTTP requests using the curl utility.
// Open the file in Wireshark on Windows and analyze it. Two HTTP curl requests were sent. The request and the server's 200 response are visible in the dump.


// Now capturing traffic from Windows. Launch Wireshark. Select the vEthernet (WSL (Hyper-V firewall)) interface.


curl -v http://172.18.0.2


// Send requests via curl again.

// The traffic dump contains no curl requests or server responses.
// This happens because the request is sent from inside WSL. The vEthernet (WSL (Hyper-V firewall)) interface is used when traffic goes outside of WSL to the host machine.



